# Charge ceiling for the ASUS ZenBook UX534FTC.
#
# WHY A UDEV RULE AND NOT A SCRIPT. `charge_control_end_threshold` is a plain
# sysfs attribute with no persistence of its own: it resets to 100 on every
# boot, so a one-shot write at apply time converges the machine exactly once and
# then quietly stops being true. udev re-applies it every time the power_supply
# device appears, which is the only place this value can live and stay lived.
#
# 80 is the ceiling. A lithium cell ages fastest held at full charge, and this
# is a laptop that spends most of its life on mains, so the top 20% is capacity
# traded for cycle life. Raise it for a trip and let this rule take it back.
#
# The `!=""` guard is not ceremony: a battery whose driver does not expose the
# attribute would otherwise make the rule fail rather than skip, and this host
# may yet see a second power_supply device that is not this battery.
#
# GATED on the `ux534` host fact via .chezmoidata/system.yaml (udev.overrides).
# The file ships to no other machine.
ACTION=="add|change", SUBSYSTEM=="power_supply", KERNEL=="BAT[0-9]", ATTR{type}=="Battery", ATTR{charge_control_end_threshold}!="", ATTR{charge_control_end_threshold}="80"
